#SERVICE PRICING
SERVICE_PRICING_INSTRUCTIONS: str = """

    The customer is asking the business, lone wraps, about a service or what it costs. That is any message about getting work done on their vehicle or the details of that work: a quote or price, what is included, colors or finishes, materials or brands, how long the install takes, how long it lasts, warranty, deposit, bringing their own vinyl, what a full wrap covers, PPF coverage (front end, the high impact add on, full body, windshield), tint shades or which windows, a ceramic coating on its own or on top of a wrap or PPF, what services the shop offers, or wanting to get work done that the shop hasn't priced yet. Lead form replies that only name the job ("Color change", "Full matte black", "Full wrap. Currently white, wanting matte black", "Clear protection", "Window tint", "Ceramic coating") and short replies that give the vehicle, color, PPF coverage or windows the shop just asked for on one of the regular services below are service and pricing too. Coming by the shop to see something for a service (wrap colors, the color books, samples, colored PPF colors, tint shades) is service and pricing too, even when it also asks when the shop is open or where it is. A general question about how one of these services or its film works, like the difference between IR and UV or how vinyl holds up in a car wash, is service and pricing too. So is something the shop doesn't do, like home window tint, headlight or taillight tint or undercoating, so the shop can tell them. Commercial wraps, custom wraps (a partial, a two tone, a specialty film like chrome, color shift or camo, or a custom design like a graphic wrap, graphics, a printed design, a livery or a fade on their own car, "Im looking to do a graphic wrap on my car"), chrome deletes, taking off or redoing PPF or a wrap that's on the vehicle now, PPF on just one piece, and jobs the services below don't list are services_req_humans, even when they only ask whether the shop does one or ask for one next to a regular service. A follow up on one of those jobs from the earlier turns is services_req_humans too, even when it only talks about the price, the budget or how many vehicles and doesn't name the job again, unless it turns the job down, which is an objection (below). On a commercial job from the earlier turns, a full wrap is one of its three jobs next to decals and a half wrap, never the vinyl wrap/color change below, so picking one of them or asking whether their prices are per vehicle is services_req_humans too, and so is every answer after it to what the shop asked on that job: the makes and models of the vans and trucks, how many there are or the logo files ("They are all transits, the trucks are f150s"). A person prices the whole fleet, so those never get a color change price. When the shop's last reply asked whether it's a full wrap, a half wrap or just decals, that's the commercial wrap's question, so the answer to it ("Full wrap", "full", "half", "just decals" and the like) is services_req_humans whatever the vehicle is (a G Wagon, a Tesla, any personal car or SUV), even when they also floated a regular vinyl wrap or color change as an option ("I want to wrap my business on there or do a vinyl wraps whatever makes more sense"). A question about whether a price the shop gave is for their business wrap ("Wait that’s the business wrap price?") goes back to the commercial wrap too, even right after the shop quoted a regular wrap. Only saying outright they're dropping the business wrap for a color change goes by the regular service.

    Detailing is the one thing the shop doesn't do that isn't service and pricing: asking only whether the shop does detailing (a detail, interior or exterior detailing, a car wash) is general_text, since that reply says no and asks what else they're looking for. Detailing asked next to one of the shop's services goes by that service.

    A message about a regular service the shop already quoted a price for in the earlier turns is booking_service_acknowledge only when it explicitly commits to getting it done or on the books: saying outright they want to go ahead with it ("ok lets do it", "lets do the camo then", "ill take the full body"), saying they're ready or asking how to book or confirm it ("I'm ready", "how do I confirm?", "I can book now and just pick a color when I come in"), asking to book it or get on the schedule (what days are open, when they can bring it in), or a clear yes to the shop asking whether they want to get on the schedule. Those are booking_service_acknowledge even when they also ask about the job or the shop, whatever words the yes is in ("yeah! how long would you need it?", "yessir, do yall take card?"), since the booking reply answers the question from the shop's info. A color they haven't picked yet never holds that back: a wrap or colored PPF is the same price in any gloss, satin or matte color and the booking step makes sure the shop has the exact color first, so it counts even when they don't name a color or the shop's last reply asked which color they picked. Everything else about a quoted job is service and pricing, even when it sounds close to ready: answering which option they're leaning towards without saying they want to go ahead ("probably the full body"), picking, adding or changing a shade, window, coverage or add on ("20% all around", "add the windshield too"), saying back what they want done or what the shop will do ("so you will do 20% on the front windshield"), any question or comment about the job, a plain ok, cool, sounds good or thanks that isn't answering the shop's schedule question, or still deciding between options. Changing the color or finish of the whole wrap ("actually can we do gloss instead of satin?") is service and pricing too, but one piece of it in its own color or finish ("can you do the roof in gloss black instead of satin?") is a two tone, which is services_req_humans. Before the shop quoted a price for it, wanting to book it or get it done is still service and pricing, so it gets priced first ("can I get my 2020 civic tinted this week?").

    An objection about a service is service and pricing too, whatever service it's about: turning down or pushing back on a service, an option or an add on that came up in the conversation, like passing on it or skipping it, going somewhere else or with another shop for it, saying they don't want it or aren't interested anymore, holding off on it, or that it costs more than they want to spend or isn't worth it to them ("Ok I will pass on the window tint and go somewhere else for that", "nah ill skip the chrome delete, thats too much", "nvm we're gonna go with someone local for the vans", "eh nevermind on the black roof, just the full wrap", "found someone cheaper, thanks anyway", "thats more than I want to spend right now, ill hold off"). That holds when what they turn down is a job services_req_humans covers (a chrome delete, a custom or commercial wrap, PPF or an old wrap coming off), and when it opens with ok, yeah or yes ("yeah im good on the coating"), since the reply takes it in stride and goes on with whatever else they're still getting. It only goes by another category when the same message also asks for a job services_req_humans covers ("I'll pass on the tint, but can you black out the chrome?") or explicitly commits to a regular service the shop already quoted ("Ill pass on the coating, just the wrap. Lets get it booked").

    Wrapping up the conversation for now, with nothing asked and nothing turned down, is end_conversation, never service and pricing: saying they'll think it over, talk it over with someone, check their budget or schedule, shop around or get back to the shop when they're ready ("ok cool, ill think about it and let you know"), putting the whole job off for now with no pushback ("ill hold off for now", "nah not right now" to the shop's schedule question), or thanking the shop for the info as a sign off ("thanks for the info!"). Holding off on one service, option or add on while the rest stays, and any pushback on a service or a price, is still an objection, and a plain ok, cool or thanks that only takes in the shop's answer still goes here too.

    A general question about the services is service and pricing too, even when it names no service: what services the shop offers, what it does or what kind of work it does ("what do yall do over there?", "what type of work does your shop do?"), whether it does more than one service ("do you guys just do tint?"), what else it offers, or its prices, deals or specials in general ("got any deals going on?"). A specific deal, promo or discount they point to, one they saw or heard about or someone at the shop offered them outside this conversation, is escalation ("I saw your post about $500 off wraps this month, is that still going?"), but a special that's part of the shop's own pricing stays here: the ceramic coating at half off on top of a wrap, or the free side and rear tint with a full body of PPF or colored PPF ("can I get a quote on that and the ceramic coating special?").

    Stars in the headliner (a starlight headliner) are something the shop doesn't do, so asking about them, on their own or next to a job ("looking to get my car wrapped & stars if you guys offer that service"), is service and pricing too, so the reply can tell them, never services_req_humans. Asking whether the shop sells something to buy, like film, vinyl, PPF or tint on its own ("you sell matte ppf?", "do you guys sell vinyl rolls?"), is general_text, since the shop doesn't sell anything and that reply says so, while asking whether it does a service ("do you do matte ppf?") stays here. A customer who wants work done on their own vehicle and also brings up a collab, a feature or content for their page goes by the job too, never escalation, since the job's reply answers the collab part ("just got a model y and want it wrapped matte brown, would love to do a collab with you guys" is service and pricing).

    A question about how the shop does the job on their vehicle is service and pricing too, like which parts come off for the install (the bumpers, headlights, taillights, handles, window trim, mirrors). So is any message that asks about one of the services below or their job and also asks something about the shop itself, like its hours, where it is, how to reach it, payments, or whether the shop is insured ("What are your hours? Also do you do ceramic coating?", "What parts would need to come off Cause it’s an expensive car Do you guys have insurance? In case you mess up a piece", "How long would you need it? And you guys are insured right?"). The service reply has the shop's info too, so it answers both. A job services_req_humans covers stays services_req_humans, shop question or not. A yes to the shop's schedule question, going ahead or asking to book, with a question about the job or the shop is booking_service_acknowledge instead ("yeah! how long would you need it?", "yessir, do yall take card?"), since the booking reply answers it too.

    Clear PPF on the front end (the front, frontal or full front PPF), the full body or the windshield is a regular service whether it goes on the paint or on top of the wrap the shop quoted them, since the film goes on over vinyl too. So picking the wrap and asking for PPF with it or over it is service and pricing ("Im thinking just cheaper one for now Can i get a frontal ppf on it To get some protection", "can you do front end ppf on top of the wrap?"). Only PPF on just one piece (just the hood, just the bumper, just the headlights) or film or a wrap that's on the vehicle now coming off is services_req_humans.

    PPF or clear bra that's on the vehicle now, in any finish (clear, satin or matte), is treated just like the regular paint when a wrap goes over it: the wrap goes right over it with no extra cost and no extra work, and it never has to come off first. So a wrap going over it is the regular vinyl wrap, service and pricing, never PPF removal, when they only say it's on there, ask whether the shop can wrap over it, whether it has to come off first or whether it costs more or takes more work ("it's got clear bra on the front, can you still wrap it?", "does the ppf need to come off before the wrap?", "is it extra to wrap over ppf?"). Only them wanting it taken off, redone or replaced is PPF removal, services_req_humans ("can you take the old ppf off before the wrap?", "the clear bra is yellowing, can you take it off and put new on?").

    PPF on certain pieces of the vehicle is services_req_humans only when they say outright that they want only those pieces covered ("just the hood", "only the bumper and headlights", "nothing else, just the mirrors"). PPF on pieces they name without saying only those (the bumper, hood and fenders, "the hood and mirrors", "can you ppf my hood, it's getting chipped up") goes to the closest coverage the shop offers, the front end, so it's service and pricing, and its reply gives the front end with what it covers. A wrap they already had taken off is just the history of the car, never wrap removal ("we had the wrap removed after it got keyed, now we want PPF on the front").

    Media goes by what they say about it and what the conversation is about, never by its service_ques alone. A photo or video of their vehicle sent to show it, with the regular service they want in their text or the earlier turns ("here she is, thinking satin black", "this is the car, how much for full front PPF?", "that's the car", or just the photo right after the shop asked what vehicle it is or what color it is now), is service and pricing, even when its service_ques says it points to a chrome delete, a commercial wrap or decals, PPF removal, wrap removal, a dent or another job off something in the picture they never asked about: tint, chrome, blacked out badges, a business logo or lettering or film already on their vehicle, the wrap that's on it now, a dent or scratch, or another vehicle's wrap in the background. So is media they point to as the look they want when it's a full wrap in one color, tint, PPF or a coating ("can you get mine this dark?", "I would like to put that color on my car"), and a photo of their vehicle sent next to it ("here's mine, want it in this").

    A problem with the business is never service and pricing: asking for a refund or their money back, a complaint about work the shop already did on their vehicle or about how the shop treated them, trouble with financing or a payment, or being angry or upset with the shop is escalation, even when the same message also asks about a service or a price ("the tint you did is already bubbling, also how much would the windshield be?"). An objection stays service and pricing however blunt it is ("thats way too much", "found someone cheaper, thanks anyway"), and so does a question about the warranty, how long a wrap or film lasts, or something normal right after an install, like haze or water bubbles in fresh tint while it cures.

    # The services are:
        1. window tint (ceramic tint on the side windows, rear glass, windshield, a visor strip (also called a brow, windshield brow, eyebrow or sun strip, the tinted strip across the top of the windshield) or a sunroof, and anything about its shades, how dark is legal, taking off or redoing old tint, bubbles after the install, warranty or turnaround)
        2. vinyl wrap/color change (a full wrap in one gloss, satin or matte color, metallic and pearl colors included, and anything about the full wrap's colors, finishes, materials, warranty, turnaround, deposit, customer supplied vinyl or PPF that's on the vehicle now going under it, which is treated just like the paint). A plain "wrap", "full wrap", "wrap my car" or "color change" with no design named is this one ("Im looking to wrap my car", "full wrap", "color change"), while a graphic wrap, graphics, a printed design, a livery, a fade or a design on their car is a custom wrap, services_req_humans
        3. paint protection film/PPF, also called clear bra or clear wrap (front end, the high impact add on, full body, windshield skin, stealth, matte or satin PPF, and anything about the film's brand, thickness, coverage, warranty, turnaround, prep or it coming off someday down the road)
        4. colored PPF, the same film in a color (colored PPF, color change PPF, PPF in a color, and anything about its colors, finishes, coverage, the tint that comes with it, warranty or turnaround)
        5. ceramic coating (a clear coating on the paint, a wrap or PPF for UV protection and keeping the vehicle cleaner, on its own or added on top of a wrap or PPF, and anything about its price, brand, how long it lasts, what it protects against, what it covers, warranty, cure time or aftercare), and paint correction, which is part of the prep before a coating, PPF or wrap
"""


#BUSINESS OPERATIONS
BUSINESS_OPERATIONS_INSTRUCTIONS: str = """

    Is this user asking the business, lone wraps, about business operations? 
    
    # The type of business operations to ask, but not limited to, are:
        1. business hours 
        2. payment methods
        3. numeber of employees
        4. payment methods 
        5. financing

    Coming by the shop to see something for a service (wrap colors, the color books, samples, colored PPF colors, tint shades) is service_pricing, not business operations. Coming by with no service reason ("can I swing by today?") is business operations.

    A general question about the business itself is business operations too: saying they have a question or a few questions for the shop without asking one yet ("I've got a few questions about the shop", "hey, question for you"), or asking to hear about the shop, the company or Lone Wraps ("can you tell me a bit about your company?"). A general question about what the shop does, the kind of work it does, or its prices, deals or specials is service_pricing.

    Whether the shop is licensed or insured is business operations too ("are you guys licensed and insured?", "do you guys have insurance in case something happens to it while its there?"). Business operations is only for a message about nothing but the shop. When the same message also asks anything about a job on their vehicle (whether the shop does a service, which parts come off, the price, the colors or how long it takes), it goes by the job: one of the regular services is service_pricing ("What are your hours? Also do you do ceramic coating?", "What parts would need to come off Cause it’s an expensive car Do you guys have insurance? In case you mess up a piece"), and a job services_req_humans covers is services_req_humans.

    Saying outright they want to go ahead with a regular service the shop already quoted a price for, asking to book it or get on the schedule, or a clear yes to the shop asking whether they want to get on the schedule is booking_service_acknowledge, even when it also asks about the shop ("lets do it, do you take cards?", "lets get it booked, do you take cards?"). Coming by with no service reason and no quoted job they're booking ("can I swing by today?") is still business operations.

    Once the shop quoted a price for a regular service, asking what the shop's schedule or availability looks like is asking what days are open for that job, so it's booking_service_acknowledge, never the hours ("Ok what’s your schedule look like", "whats your availability like this week?"). With no regular service quoted in the earlier turns, the shop's schedule is its hours, so it's business operations ("what's your schedule like?" with nothing before it). Asking when the shop opens or closes ("what time do you close today?", "are you open saturdays?") is still business operations, quoted job or not, as long as nothing in the message is about a job on their vehicle.

    Service and pricing always wins over the shop's info. When the same message asks about the shop (whether it's open or available on a day, the hours, where it is, payments) and also brings up a job on their vehicle, in any order, it goes by the job: service_pricing for a regular service, services_req_humans for a job a person prices ("hey are you guys available this friday? got a new audi i want wrapped", "are you open saturday? need tint on my tacoma"). The job's reply answers the shop part too.

    A shop question that only names a service in general, with no job on their own vehicle in it, is still business operations, like financing or paying for a wrap ("can I finance a wrap with you guys?", "do you take card for tint?"). Its reply answers it and asks whether they're looking to get that done.

    Trouble with financing or a payment they already tried is escalation, not business operations: Affirm denying them or not going through, a payment that won't go through, being charged twice or charged wrong, or the invoice or the estimate being wrong ("Affirm denied me, what do I do now?", "I got charged twice for the deposit"). So is a complaint about how the shop treated them, like nobody getting back to them, and being angry or upset with the shop. Asking whether the shop does financing, how it works or what payments it takes is still business operations. Whether the shop is hiring or wanting to work there, and someone selling something to the shop or proposing a business deal, is escalation too ("are you guys hiring?", "Im with Apex Marketing, we help wrap shops get more leads"), while how many people work at the shop is still business operations.
"""


#HUMAN SERVICES
